# The sensors HAL is sensors.tegra, a blob, and what it needs falls into two
# halves that deserve to be read differently.

# The first half is ordinary. The sensors are IIO devices: samples come off
# the character devices, and everything that configures them -- rates, batch
# sizes, which axes are enabled, the names by which they are recognised --
# is on the first I2C bus, under one label for the whole bus.
allow hal_sensors_default iio_device:chr_file rw_file_perms;

allow hal_sensors_default sysfs_nvi2c:dir r_dir_perms;
allow hal_sensors_default sysfs_nvi2c:file rw_file_perms;

# It finds them by listing /sys/bus/iio/devices, and reads the input device's
# name to tell one board from another.
allow hal_sensors_default sysfs:dir r_dir_perms;
allow hal_sensors_default sysfs:file r_file_perms;

# The second half is the blob's files, at paths built into sensors.lsm6db0:
# the calibration it keeps in /data/sensors (gyro.dat, acc.dat, magn.dat;
# init.tn8.rc makes the directory) and the named pipes it carries samples
# through between its own threads, in /data/sns_lsm6db0_pipes, a directory
# it makes itself straight under /data at every start.
#
# Both get a type of their own, labelled by path.
#
# The blob may not make the pipes' directory itself: S lets no one but init
# add or remove an entry straight under /data (system/sepolicy/public/init.te,
# the neverallow on system_data_root_file). The directory has to be there
# already, made by init, and the blob's attempt to make it again finds it.

allow hal_sensors_default sensors_data_file:dir create_dir_perms;
allow hal_sensors_default sensors_data_file:file create_file_perms;
allow hal_sensors_default sensors_data_file:fifo_file create_file_perms;

# What is deliberately not granted is how the blob clears the pipes at
# start: it shells out to rm, and the boot log is full of it being denied a
# shell and toybox.
#
# That is not an oversight. The platform forbids it outright -- no HAL may
# execute a program without changing domain, with exactly two exemptions
# named in hal_neverallows.te, and neither is a sensor driver. Writing the
# rule does not produce a wider policy, it produces a policy that does not
# build.
#
# The legitimate form is a helper domain: a transition on the shell, so that
# whatever the blob runs lands somewhere that may touch the pipes and nothing
# else. That is a domain to design rather than a rule to add, and it is worth
# doing when this blob is replaced or when this board goes enforcing. Until
# then, under enforcing the rm would be refused, the pipes of the previous
# start would stay, and making them again would find them already there.

# Our own HAL, sensors-subhal.mocha under the multi-HAL, in the same domain.
#
# It keeps the hub's calibration in /data/vendor/sensors, and watches the
# directory for the accelerometer's calibration the settings write there, to
# take it as soon as it lands.
allow hal_sensors_default sensors_vendor_data_file:dir { create_dir_perms watch };
allow hal_sensors_default sensors_vendor_data_file:file create_file_perms;

# The ambient light sensor reports through the input layer; the HAL holds its
# event node open and sets the clock its events are stamped with.
allow hal_sensors_default input_device:dir r_dir_perms;
allow hal_sensors_default input_device:chr_file r_file_perms;
